<GovernanceLayout
  title="Editorial Standards"
  description="The editorial standards that govern every page Commercial Roofing Intel publishes: sourcing, review, independence, AI use, and update discipline."
  crumbName="Editorial Standards"
  lede="These standards are binding on everything CRI publishes. If a page fails them, it does not ship — and if you find a page that fails them, we want to know."
  updated="2026-07-15"
>
  <h2>1. Sourcing</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Every factual claim must trace to a verifiable source: standards bodies (e.g., NRCA, ASTM, FM Approvals, UL), building codes, peer-reviewed research, manufacturer technical data, government data, or CRI’s own published datasets.</li>
    <li>Marketing materials are not acceptable sources for performance claims.</li>
    <li>Sources are cited on the page. If we cannot source a claim, we do not publish it.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>2. Review</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Every page is reviewed before publication for factual accuracy, completeness, and clarity.</li>
    <li>Technical pages receive subject-matter review by a qualified reviewer, named or described on the page.</li>
    <li>Every page displays its last-reviewed date and is re-reviewed on a scheduled cycle (annually at minimum; sooner when codes, standards, or evidence change).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>3. Independence</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>No advertiser, contractor, manufacturer, or referral partner reviews, approves, or influences editorial content before publication.</li>
    <li>Editorial staff compensation is never tied to referral revenue.</li>
    <li>Products and companies are named only where evidence supports it, and comparisons follow the published <a href="/about/methodology/">Methodology</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>4. Uncertainty and limitations</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Where evidence is mixed, condition-dependent, or evolving, the page must say so explicitly.</li>
    <li>Tools and datasets state their assumptions, coverage, and limitations alongside their outputs.</li>
    <li>CRI content is educational. It is not engineering, legal, or insurance advice, and pages remind readers where professional judgment is required.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>5. Use of AI</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>AI systems may assist with drafting, structuring, and consistency checking.</li>
    <li>No AI-generated claim is published without human verification against the sourcing standard above.</li>
    <li>The CRI AI Assistant answers only from approved, published CRI sources and discloses gaps rather than improvising.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>6. Corrections</h2>
  <p>
    Errors are corrected promptly and transparently under our
    <a href="/about/corrections/">Corrections policy</a>. Material corrections are noted on
    the page itself.
  </p>
</GovernanceLayout>
